<!-- 14. FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<section class="py-20 md:py-28 bg-graphite" aria-labelledby="faq-heading">
  <div class="container-x">
    <SectionHeading
      id="faq-heading"
      index="14"
      label="Frequently Asked Questions"
      title="Common questions about SRE and operations."
      lede="Answers to the questions we hear most from engineering leaders evaluating SRE adoption."
      tone="graphite"
    />

    <div class="mt-14 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2" data-reveal-group>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-graphite-line bg-graphite-2 p-6" data-reveal>
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-graphite-fg mb-2">How long does it take to adopt SRE?</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-graphite-fg-2">SRE adoption is a journey, not a project. Most organizations see meaningful improvements within three months of implementing SLOs and structured incident response. Full cultural transformation typically takes six to twelve months depending on organizational size and maturity.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-graphite-line bg-graphite-2 p-6" data-reveal>
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-graphite-fg mb-2">Do we need a dedicated SRE team?</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-graphite-fg-2">Not initially. Many organizations start with embedded SRE practitioners within existing teams and evolve to a dedicated function as the practice matures. The right structure depends on your team size, system complexity, and reliability requirements.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-graphite-line bg-graphite-2 p-6" data-reveal>
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-graphite-fg mb-2">What is the difference between SLO and SLA?</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-graphite-fg-2">An SLO is an internal target for service reliability, defined by your engineering team. An SLA is a contractual commitment to customers. SLOs are typically stricter than SLAs to provide early warning before contractual breach. We recommend setting SLOs that give at least one error budget window of warning before SLA violation.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-graphite-line bg-graphite-2 p-6" data-reveal>
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-graphite-fg mb-2">How do you define meaningful SLIs?</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-graphite-fg-2">Good SLIs are user-facing, measurable, and actionable. We start by asking: what does user happiness look like for this service? Latency, error rate, and availability are common starting points. Every SLI should have a clear relationship to the user experience and be measurable with existing or easily added instrumentation.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-graphite-line bg-graphite-2 p-6" data-reveal>
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-graphite-fg mb-2">What is an error budget and how does it work?</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-graphite-fg-2">An error budget is 1 minus your SLO over a time window. For a 99.9% monthly SLO, you have 43 minutes of allowable unreliability per month. When the budget is available, teams can deploy features freely. When it is depleted, releases pause and reliability work takes priority. This removes subjective decisions about when to prioritize stability over features.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-graphite-line bg-graphite-2 p-6" data-reveal>
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-graphite-fg mb-2">How do you measure incident response effectiveness?</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-graphite-fg-2">Key metrics include: Mean Time To Detect, Mean Time To Respond, Mean Time To Mitigate, and Mean Time To Recover. We also track incident frequency by severity, percentage of incidents with post-incident reviews, and action item closure rate. The goal is year-over-year reduction in both frequency and impact.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-graphite-line bg-graphite-2 p-6" data-reveal>
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-graphite-fg mb-2">What is the ideal on-call rotation?</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-graphite-fg-2">We recommend secondary rotations of one week with at least three people per rotation to ensure sustainability. Primary and secondary responder models provide fallback. Frequency depends on team size and incident volume. The goal is balance: enough coverage for reliable response without burning out engineers.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-graphite-line bg-graphite-2 p-6" data-reveal>
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-graphite-fg mb-2">How do you handle Kubernetes cluster operations?</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-graphite-fg-2">We follow a structured approach: declarative cluster provisioning with Terraform or Cluster API, GitOps for application configuration, automated upgrades, security hardening with Pod Security Standards and network policies, resource quotas and limits, and comprehensive observability with Prometheus and Grafana. Day-2 operations are automated where possible.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-graphite-line bg-graphite-2 p-6" data-reveal>
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-graphite-fg mb-2">What availability should we target?</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-graphite-fg-2">The right availability target depends on user expectations, business impact of downtime, and engineering cost. Five nines (99.999%) is exponentially more expensive than three nines (99.9%). We help you determine the optimal target by analyzing user behavior, revenue impact, and operational cost at each availability level.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-graphite-line bg-graphite-2 p-6" data-reveal>
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-graphite-fg mb-2">How do you approach disaster recovery?</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-graphite-fg-2">We start with business requirements: Recovery Time Objective and Recovery Point Objective for each critical system. Then we design the appropriate architecture: backup and restore, pilot light, warm standby, or active-active. Every DR plan is documented, automated, and tested regularly with scheduled drills.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-graphite-line bg-graphite-2 p-6" data-reveal>
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-graphite-fg mb-2">Can SRE work in a regulated industry?</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-graphite-fg-2">Yes. SRE practices are compatible with regulatory frameworks like SOC 2, HIPAA, PCI DSS, and FedRAMP. In fact, the automation, change management, and observability that SRE provides make compliance easier to achieve and audit. We have experience implementing SRE in the most regulated environments.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-graphite-line bg-graphite-2 p-6" data-reveal>
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-graphite-fg mb-2">What is the difference between monitoring and observability?</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-graphite-fg-2">Monitoring tells you when something is wrong. Observability lets you understand why. Monitoring is dashboards and alerts for known failure modes. Observability is the ability to explore unknown unknowns by querying rich telemetry data. Both are necessary. Observability enables faster root cause analysis and more confident debugging.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-graphite-line bg-graphite-2 p-6" data-reveal>
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-graphite-fg mb-2">How do you measure reliability improvement?</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-graphite-fg-2">We track a balanced set of metrics: SLO attainment, error budget burn rate, MTTR, deployment frequency, change failure rate, and toil percentage. Quarterly reliability reviews compare these metrics against baselines and targets. The goal is continuous improvement, not perfection.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-graphite-line bg-graphite-2 p-6" data-reveal>
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-graphite-fg mb-2">What does a managed SRE service include?</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-graphite-fg-2">24/7 monitoring and alerting, incident response and on-call coverage, SLO monitoring and reporting, capacity planning, release support, regular reliability reviews, and continuous improvement initiatives. Services are delivered against defined SLAs with monthly reporting and quarterly business reviews.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-graphite-line bg-graphite-2 p-6" data-reveal>
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-graphite-fg mb-2">How do you handle capacity planning?</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-graphite-fg-2">We use a data-driven approach combining trend analysis, usage forecasting, and business input. Monitor resource utilization trends, model growth scenarios, set provisioning thresholds, and automate scaling. Regular capacity reviews ensure resources are right-sized and cost-optimized.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-graphite-line bg-graphite-2 p-6" data-reveal>
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-graphite-fg mb-2">What is chaos engineering and do we need it?</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-graphite-fg-2">Chaos engineering is the practice of proactively testing system resilience by injecting failures in a controlled manner. It is valuable for organizations with complex distributed systems where failure modes are difficult to predict. We start with low-risk experiments in non-production environments and gradually expand scope as confidence grows.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-graphite-line bg-graphite-2 p-6" data-reveal>
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-graphite-fg mb-2">How do you reduce alert fatigue?</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-graphite-fg-2">We apply a systematic approach: eliminate noisy alerts by tuning thresholds, aggregate related alerts, implement alert deduplication, define clear severity levels, ensure every alert is actionable, and route alerts to the right team. We also implement burn rate alerts on SLOs that provide early warning without noise.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-graphite-line bg-graphite-2 p-6" data-reveal>
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-graphite-fg mb-2">What is the role of AI in SRE?</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-graphite-fg-2">AI and ML enhance SRE practices through anomaly detection, intelligent alert correlation, automated root cause analysis, and predictive capacity planning. We integrate AI capabilities where they provide clear value, but foundational SRE practices (SLOs, error budgets, automation) remain the primary drivers of reliability.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-graphite-line bg-graphite-2 p-6" data-reveal>
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-graphite-fg mb-2">How do you handle multi-cloud operations?</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-graphite-fg-2">We use cloud-agnostic tooling and abstractions: Terraform or Pulumi for infrastructure, Kubernetes for compute, Prometheus and OpenTelemetry for observability. Each cloud provider is managed through consistent practices with provider-specific optimizations where they add value. The goal is portability without sacrificing native capabilities.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-graphite-line bg-graphite-2 p-6" data-reveal>
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-graphite-fg mb-2">How do you ensure knowledge transfer?</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-graphite-fg-2">Knowledge transfer is built into every engagement. We document systems and processes, create runbooks, work in pairs with your engineers, conduct training sessions, and establish documentation standards. Our goal is to make your team fully self-sufficient. We measure success by your team's ability to operate independently.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

